Context: in 'Analysis situs (4e mouture)' Grothendieck works with a set L and the set Figures(L) of 'figures' in L. A figure is a collection of subsets, the 'strata' or '(multi)structures'. These figures carry:
- a product •;
- an interior X° for each multistructure X;
- a relation X |∘| Y, read as 'X° ∩ Y° = ∅', i.e. disjointness;
- a relation Y ∘⊂ X, meaning Y° ⊂ X°.

Previous page: for a set map f : L' → L he defines the pull-back f*(𝔏) = { f⁻¹(A) | A ∈ 𝔏, A° ∩ L0 ≠ ∅ }, where L0 = f(L'). He factors it in three steps:
- (**) restriction X ↦ F_{L0}•X, from Figures(L) to Figures(L)_{L0}, the figures located in L0; here F_{L0} = {L0} is the one-stratum figure;
- (***) the identification Figures(L)_{L0} ≅ Figures(L0);
- f0*, the pull-back along the surjection f0 : L' → L0. Because f0 is surjective, the non-emptiness condition becomes automatic.

This page starts by finishing formula (2): it holds 'for every A ∈ 𝔏0'. Then:

(i) The restriction (**) is read as a 'passage to the quotient'. In Fig(L) one formally sets X = ∅ for every multistructure X with X° ∩ L0 = ∅, i.e. F_{L0} |∘| X. Equivalently, one keeps exactly the substructures Y with Y° ∩ L0 ≠ ∅.

(ii) Speculative generalisation ('on devine'). Such a quotient should make sense whenever one chooses a part M that is '|∘|-closed', called a '|∘|-support', whose elements are to be neglected (set to 0). Neglecting X does not mean neglecting all its substructures, but at least all Y with Y ∘⊂ X, i.e. Y° ⊂ X°. So M must be a down-set (an ideal) for the order 'inclusion of interiors'. In modern terms this is a quotient of an ordered/semiring-like structure by an ideal, analogous to quotienting a Boolean algebra or distributive lattice by an ideal, or restricting to an open/closed piece of a space.

(iii) Restricted to Figures(L)_{L0}, the map (**) is an isomorphism. It is inverse to Figures(L0) ≅ Figures(L)_{L0} ↪ Figures(L). Hence the composite i_* : Figures(L0) → Figures(L), the push-forward along the inclusion i : L0 ↪ L, is a 'plongement' (embedding) with image Figures(L)_{L0}.

Purpose: this sets up a functorial pair f*, i_* on the lattices of figures. The next page uses it to note:
- i* i_* = id and i_* i*(X) = i_*(1)·X;
- a projection formula i_*(X')·Y = i_*(X'·i*(Y));
- compatibility with Sup's and Inf's.
This is a small 'six-operations-like' formalism for a combinatorial analysis situs.
